---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { links } from '../config';

/*
 * The one page on the site written in the first person.
 *
 * Everywhere else explains the product; this explains the people. Different
 * job, so a different voice: past tense, "we wondered", the things that went
 * wrong left in. The bench-week details are lifted from docs/roadmap.md's
 * "What live testing taught us" -- they are the texture that makes it read as
 * something that actually happened, because it did.
 *
 * Nothing invented. Every specific here traces to the repo.
 */
---

<Layout
  title="Why"
  description="Why DocSigner exists: no open-source tool would sign with a DSC token, and the first week on a real bench taught us why that is harder than it looks."
>
  <Hero kicker="Why" title="Why we built this" lede="Short version: nothing else would do it." />

  <Section ground="deep">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I am a chartered accountant who ended up writing software. Most days that
        means Frappe and ERPNext, and a lot of what I build ends in the same
        place: a document nobody will accept until it has been signed.
      </p>
      <p>
        In India that means a DSC on a USB token. Almost everyone doing this work
        has one in a drawer. What none of us had was a decent way to use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        So we went looking. Every tool that handled tokens properly was
        commercial, or Windows-only, or both. The open-source ones signed happily
        with a key in a file, which is not where a DSC lives. That left paying,
        rebooting into Windows, or printing the page and signing it by hand.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">We wondered how hard it could really be.</p>
      <p>
        Harder than it looks. Though almost none of the hard part turned out to be
        cryptography.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="The first week" title="What a real token taught us">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        It worked against test keys long before it worked against hardware. Then
        we plugged in a real Capricorn token, and the list of things we had wrong
        got long quickly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="lessons">
      <li>
        <h3>An empty list has to explain itself</h3>
        <p>
          Ours said “plug in your token” no matter what was actually wrong. It
          reports the readers it can see now, so a missing token and a token with
          no driver read differently. That one cost an afternoon of guessing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Token drivers wedge</h3>
        <p>
          The ePass and ProxKey families let exactly one process talk to the token
          at a time, and some only list certificates at the moment they are
          initialised. So the helper re-initialises on every scan, gives each
          driver twenty seconds, and names the program you need to close.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Ten handwriting fonts was nine too many</h3>
        <p>
          We shipped ten. Nobody wants to audition ten fonts to sign an invoice.
          There are five now, one per personality.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="after">
      None of that is in a specification. You find it by plugging the thing in,
      which is the only reason it is worth writing down.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section ground="deep" label="One more reason" title="The paper">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every signature made this way is one less page printed and posted. Next to
        everything above that is a small thing, and it is still one of the
        reasons we kept going.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section centre>
    <div class="end">
      <h2>All of it is Apache-2.0</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Including the parts that touch your token. Read it before you run it.
      </p>
      <div class="row">
        <Button href="/download" variant="primary" size="lg">Download</Button>
        <Button href={links.repo} variant="default" size="lg" trailing="lucide:external-link">
          Read the source
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  /* One line gets to be the turn in the story. */
  .pull {
    font-size: var(--display-3);
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--text);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    margin-block: var(--s-2);
  }

  .lessons {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: l;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-6);
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
  }

  .lessons li {
    counter-increment: l;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 26px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-3);
  }

  .lessons li::before {
    content: counter(l);
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    border-radius: var(--pill);
    background: var(--green-soft);
    color: var(--green-ink);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .lessons h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--s-1);
  }

  .lessons p {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  .after {
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
    padding-left: var(--s-4);
    border-left: var(--accent-bar) solid var(--green);
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .end {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    justify-content: center;
  }
</style>
